<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

From top to bottom:

1. An optional header widget.
2. A card title, with an optional icon and, at the right, a header switch that turns the switchable rows on or off together. Home Assistant shows the switch by default when the card has a title and at least two switchable rows. `show_header_toggle: false` hides it.
3. The rows, one under another, with a gap of 8 pixels.
4. An optional footer widget.

Each entity row has a round state icon, the row name, an optional line of secondary information and, on the right, the control or value for that kind of entity. A row with an action is a tab stop, and its focus shows as a change of background colour. When the entity cannot be found, the row shows a warning.

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-hui-entities-card"><code>hui-entities-card</code></h3>

A Lovelace card (`type: entities`). `entities` is required.

```yaml
type: entities
title: Living room
entities:
  - sensor.living_room_temperature
  - entity: light.ceiling
    name: Ceiling light
    secondary_info: last-changed
  - type: section
    label: Doors
  - lock.front_door
```

Card keys: `title`, `entities`, `show_header_toggle`, `theme`, `icon`, `header`, `footer` and `color`. The older `state_color` key still works but is replaced by `color`. A row can be a plain entity ID or an object with `entity`, `name`, `icon`, `secondary_info`, `type`, `action_name`, `tap_action`, `hold_action` and `double_tap_action`. The older `service` and `service_data` keys are replaced by `action` and `data`.

Home Assistant draws the card title as a first-level heading, so give each entities card a short title that names the group.

**What the Civica theme sets.** The card uses the theme’s square corners, 1 pixel border and no shadow. Row names use `primary-text-color` and secondary information uses `secondary-text-color`. Dividers use `divider-color`. State icons use `state-icon-color`, with `state-active-color` for an active state, `state-inactive-color` for an inactive one and `state-icon-error-color` for an error state. Warnings use `warning-color` and errors use `error-color`.

**What stays as Home Assistant draws it.** The switch, slider and button shapes inside rows are Home Assistant’s own. The row gap uses `--entities-card-row-gap`, which the theme does not set, so it stays at 8 pixels. Changing it needs the card-mod integration, for example on `hui-entities-card #states`.

**Rows Home Assistant chooses for you.** If a row has no `type`, Home Assistant picks one from the domain. Domains with no row of their own get the simple row. The fan, date, datetime, number, select and time domains each get the row of the same name, described below.

<h3 id="child-hui-entities-card--hui-sensor-entity-row"><code>hui-sensor-entity-row</code></h3>

`type: sensor-entity`. Shows a reading with its unit on the right. A sensor whose device class is a timestamp shows the time instead. Sensors and any entity that only reports a value. Say what is measured in the row name.

<h3 id="child-hui-entities-card--hui-toggle-entity-row"><code>hui-toggle-entity-row</code></h3>

`type: toggle-entity`. Shows a switch. Home Assistant uses it for lights, switches, input booleans, automations, sirens, remotes, alerts and vacuums. Anything that is only on or off. Show the state in words when a wrong guess matters, such as “Unlocked” for a door.

<h3 id="child-hui-entities-card--hui-cover-entity-row"><code>hui-cover-entity-row</code></h3>

`type: cover-entity`. Shows the cover’s controls, or tilt controls when the cover can only tilt. Blinds, garage doors and curtains. Name the cover, not the action.

<h3 id="child-hui-entities-card--hui-climate-entity-row"><code>hui-climate-entity-row</code></h3>

`type: climate-entity`. Shows the state of a thermostat. Home Assistant also uses this row for water heaters. One thermostat per row. Show the unit with every temperature.

<h3 id="child-hui-entities-card--hui-humidifier-entity-row"><code>hui-humidifier-entity-row</code></h3>

`type: humidifier-entity`. Shows the state of a humidifier. One humidifier per row. Show the target as a percentage.

<h3 id="child-hui-entities-card--hui-input-number-entity-row"><code>hui-input-number-entity-row</code></h3>

`type: input-number-entity`. Shows a slider or a number box, depending on the helper’s mode, with the helper’s own minimum, maximum and step. Settings people adjust. Show the unit and the limits in the label or hint.

<h3 id="child-hui-entities-card--hui-input-select-entity-row"><code>hui-input-select-entity-row</code></h3>

`type: input-select-entity`. Shows a drop-down list of the helper’s options. A short, fixed set of choices. Word each option so it reads on its own.

<h3 id="child-hui-entities-card--hui-input-text-entity-row"><code>hui-input-text-entity-row</code></h3>

`type: input-text-entity`. Shows a text field that follows the helper’s minimum and maximum length and its text or password mode. An empty field shows Home Assistant’s own empty-value wording. Short text such as a name or a note. Keep the row name as the label.

<h3 id="child-hui-entities-card--hui-input-datetime-entity-row"><code>hui-input-datetime-entity-row</code></h3>

`type: input-datetime-entity`. Shows a date input, a time input or both, depending on what the helper holds. A single date or time. Say the time zone when it matters.

<h3 id="child-hui-entities-card--hui-input-button-entity-row"><code>hui-input-button-entity-row</code></h3>

`type: input-button-entity`. Shows a Press button. `action_name` changes the button text, and `confirmation` asks people to confirm first. One action with no state. Name the outcome, such as “Restart the router”.

<h3 id="child-hui-entities-card--hui-lock-entity-row"><code>hui-lock-entity-row</code></h3>

`type: lock-entity`. Shows a Lock or Unlock button. `confirmation` asks people to confirm first. Locks. Write the state in words in the row, not by colour alone.

<h3 id="child-hui-entities-card--hui-media-player-entity-row"><code>hui-media-player-entity-row</code></h3>

`type: media-player-entity`. Shows power, previous, play, pause, stop and next buttons and a volume control, depending on what the player supports. One player per row. The buttons are icon buttons, so keep the player name clear.

<h3 id="child-hui-entities-card--hui-scene-entity-row"><code>hui-scene-entity-row</code></h3>

`type: scene-entity`. Shows an Activate button. `action_name` changes the text and `confirmation` asks people to confirm first. Scenes. Name the scene for what it does, such as “Evening”.

<h3 id="child-hui-entities-card--hui-script-entity-row"><code>hui-script-entity-row</code></h3>

`type: script-entity`. Shows a Run button, and a Cancel button while the script is running. `action_name` changes the text and `confirmation` asks people to confirm first. Scripts. Name the outcome, not the script.

<h3 id="child-hui-entities-card--hui-timer-entity-row"><code>hui-timer-entity-row</code></h3>

`type: timer-entity`. Shows the time remaining on a timer. Timers. Say what the timer is for in the row name.

<h3 id="child-hui-entities-card--hui-update-entity-row"><code>hui-update-entity-row</code></h3>

`type: update-entity`. Shows the update state in words, for example whether an update is available. Software and device updates.

<h3 id="child-hui-entities-card--hui-valve-entity-row"><code>hui-valve-entity-row</code></h3>

`type: valve-entity`. Shows a switch to open or close a valve. Valves. Keep the state in words in the row.

<h3 id="child-hui-entities-card--hui-weather-entity-row"><code>hui-weather-entity-row</code></h3>

`type: weather-entity`. Shows the weather icon, the name and the current temperature, with a short forecast when one is available. One weather entity. Say the place in the row name.

<h3 id="child-hui-entities-card--hui-text-entity-row"><code>hui-text-entity-row</code></h3>

`type: text-entity`. Shows a text field for a text entity. Short text values. Keep the row name as the label.

<h3 id="child-hui-entities-card--hui-simple-entity-row"><code>hui-simple-entity-row</code></h3>

`type: simple-entity`. Shows the entity’s state as text. Home Assistant uses it for any domain without a row of its own. Read-only values. It is the fallback, so check the row reads well.

<h3 id="child-hui-entities-card--hui-group-entity-row"><code>hui-group-entity-row</code></h3>

`type: group-entity`. Shows a switch when every member of the group can be switched, otherwise the group’s state as text. Groups. Say what the group covers in its name.

<h3 id="child-hui-entities-card--hui-event-entity-row"><code>hui-event-entity-row</code></h3>

`type: event-entity`. Shows when the event last happened and its event type. Doorbells and similar events. Unavailable and unknown states show Home Assistant’s state wording instead of a time.

<h3 id="child-hui-entities-card--hui-button-entity-row"><code>hui-button-entity-row</code></h3>

`type: button-entity`. Shows a Press button. `action_name` changes the text and `confirmation` asks people to confirm first. One action with no state. Name the outcome.

<h3 id="child-hui-entities-card--hui-date-entity-row"><code>hui-date-entity-row</code></h3>

`type: date-entity`. Shows a date field for a date entity. Choosing a date sets the entity. The field is turned off when the entity is unavailable and is empty when its state is unknown. A date that people set, such as a collection day. Keep the row name as the label.

<h3 id="child-hui-entities-card--hui-datetime-entity-row"><code>hui-datetime-entity-row</code></h3>

`type: datetime-entity`. Shows a date field and a time field together for a date-and-time entity. The row name labels both fields, and no separate name is drawn beside them. Changing either field sets the whole value. Both fields are turned off when the entity is unavailable and are empty when its state is unknown. One moment in time. Make the row name say which moment it is.

<h3 id="child-hui-entities-card--hui-fan-entity-row"><code>hui-fan-entity-row</code></h3>

`type: fan-entity`. Shows a switch for a fan that can be turned on and off. A fan that cannot be switched, or whose state is neither on nor off, shows its state as text instead. A speed slider appears only for a fan that sets its speed in more than six steps and cannot be turned on or off. The slider sets the speed as a percentage and, when the row is wide enough, shows the state beside it. Fans. Show the state in words, not by the slider position alone.

<h3 id="child-hui-entities-card--hui-number-entity-row"><code>hui-number-entity-row</code></h3>

`type: number-entity`. Shows a slider when the entity’s mode is slider, or when its mode is automatic and its range has 256 steps or fewer. Otherwise it shows a number box with the unit after it. Both use the entity’s own minimum, maximum and step, and both are turned off when the entity is unavailable. When the row is wide enough, the slider shows its value beside it, or a dash when the state is unavailable or unknown. Settings people adjust. Show the unit and the limits in the label or hint.

<h3 id="child-hui-entities-card--hui-select-entity-row"><code>hui-select-entity-row</code></h3>

`type: select-entity`. Shows a drop-down list of the entity’s own options, with the row name as the list’s label and no separate name beside it. Each option is written in Home Assistant’s wording for that state. The list is turned off when the entity is unavailable. `entity` is required. A short, fixed set of choices. Word each option so it reads on its own.

<h3 id="child-hui-entities-card--hui-time-entity-row"><code>hui-time-entity-row</code></h3>

`type: time-entity`. Shows a time field for a time entity. Choosing a time sets the entity. The field is turned off when the entity is unavailable and is empty when its state is unknown. A time of day that people set. Keep the row name as the label.

<h3 id="child-hui-entities-card--hui-divider-row"><code>hui-divider-row</code></h3>

`type: divider`. Draws a thin line between rows. Its `style` key can set the line’s style. The line uses `divider-color`, or `entities-divider-color` if you set it. Use it to separate groups. It has no name, so it is only a visual break; use a section row when the group needs a name.

<h3 id="xref-hui-entities-card--hui-section-row"><code>hui-section-row</code></h3>

`type: section`. Draws a line with a label under it. `label` is the section name. Use it to name a group of rows. Keep the label short. See [Card & Section Header](card-and-section-header.html#child-hui-section-row) for its colours and limits.

<h3 id="child-hui-entities-card--hui-weblink-row"><code>hui-weblink-row</code></h3>

`type: weblink`. Shows a link with an icon. `url` is required. `name`, `icon`, `new_tab` and `download` are optional. Without a name, the link text is the address itself. Addresses that could run script are refused. The link text uses `primary-color`. Give the link a name that says where it goes. A link that opens a new tab should say so.

<h3 id="child-hui-entities-card--hui-attribute-row"><code>hui-attribute-row</code></h3>

`type: attribute`. Shows one attribute of an entity. `entity` and `attribute` are required. `prefix` and `suffix` add text before and after the value, and `time_format` shows a date attribute as a time. An attribute that is missing shows a dash. Use a suffix for the unit. A dash is not an explanation, so name the attribute in the row.

<h3 id="child-hui-entities-card--hui-buttons-row"><code>hui-buttons-row</code></h3>

`type: buttons`. Shows several entities as a row of buttons. `entities` is required. A switchable entity toggles when pressed and any other entity opens its More info. Holding a button opens More info. Keep it to a few buttons and give each a name or icon that says what it controls.

<h3 id="child-hui-entities-card--hui-cast-row"><code>hui-cast-row</code></h3>

`type: cast`. Shows a Cast control. Home Assistant shows “Cast requires HTTPS” or “Cast API unavailable” when it cannot cast, and “No devices found” when there is nothing to cast to. `name`, `icon`, `view` and `dashboard` are optional, and `hide_if_unavailable` hides the row when casting is not possible. Use `hide_if_unavailable` so people are not shown a control that cannot work.

<h3 id="child-hui-entities-card--hui-conditional-row"><code>hui-conditional-row</code></h3>

`type: conditional`. Wraps another row and shows it only when its conditions are met. `row` and `conditions` are required. The editor preview shows the row either way. Say elsewhere why a row may be missing, so people are not left looking for it.

<h3 id="child-hui-entities-card--hui-call-service-row"><code>hui-call-service-row</code></h3>

`type: call-service`. Shows a button that runs an action. `name` and `action` are required, and `data` gives the action its values. `perform-action` is the same row under its newer name. The older `service` key still works. Name the button for its outcome. Use a confirmation where a wrong press has a cost.

<h3 id="child-hui-entities-card--hui-button-row"><code>hui-button-row</code></h3>

`type: button`. Shows an icon, a name and a button that reads Run. `name` or `entity` is required, and `icon` and `action_name` are optional; `action_name` changes the button text. A tap toggles an entity that can be switched and opens More info for any other entity. Set `tap_action` to run something else. A hold opens More info, and `double_tap_action` is available. It has no state of its own. Use it to run a chosen action from a row, and name the outcome. For an action with fixed values, see the [call-service row](#child-hui-entities-card--hui-call-service-row) above.

<h3 id="child-hui-entities-card--hui-text-row"><code>hui-text-row</code></h3>

`type: text`. Shows an icon, a name and a line of fixed text on the right. `name` and `text` are required and `icon` is optional. The text is typed into the card, so it never follows an entity. A name or text that is too long is cut off with an ellipsis. Short notes beside a name. Keep both short, and do not put anything people must read in the cut-off part.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- One native card gives a compact, scannable list of controls and readings, with the right control for each kind of entity.
- The rows follow the household’s language, units and entity names.
- The switch, slider and button shapes cannot be changed with the theme.
- State and action are drawn by each row, so a long mixed list can be hard to scan. Use section rows and short lists.
- A divider row carries no name. A conditional row can make a row disappear without explanation.
- Rows can be changed with the theme only through colour and surface keys. Row spacing needs card-mod.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

Keep a card to one subject and a short list of rows. Make each row name match the entity’s own name, so what people read on the card is what they see in settings. Write state and unit as words, such as “Locked” and “20.5°C”, not as colour or an icon alone. Give an action button a name that says its outcome. Use `confirmation` where a wrong press matters.

Use a [summary list](summary-list.html) for plain names and values that do nothing. Use [Entity lists, readings and row actions](../home-assistant/entity-lists-and-rows.html) to decide which row fits each entity.

Success criteria that apply:

- 1.3.1 Info and Relationships: each row’s name and value belong together. Test the card’s headings and list structure with a screen reader in your own dashboard.
- 1.4.1 Use of Colour: state is written in words as well as shown by an icon colour.
- 1.4.3 and 1.4.11 Contrast: test row text, icons and dividers against the card background in your own dashboard, in light and dark modes.
- 1.4.10 Reflow: test the card at 320 CSS pixels.
- 2.1.1 Keyboard and 2.4.7 Focus Visible: a row with an action is a tab stop. Test every row’s focus in your own dashboard.
- 2.5.3 Label in Name and 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value: test the names of switches, selects and buttons.
- 2.5.8 Target Size (Minimum): test switches and buttons by touch.
- 3.3.2 Labels or Instructions: an input row needs its name as the label.

